WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.460
Your heart muscle cell and a white fat cell share many of the same kinds of parts.

2
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.660
So why do they look nothing alike inside?

3
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.580
Let's build a cell and find out.

4
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:16.773
Start with the outline.

5
00:00:18.093 --> 00:00:21.133
The cell membrane is the thin boundary around the cell.

6
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:24.273
It controls what enters and leaves.

7
00:00:26.073 --> 00:00:31.073
Inside is the cytoplasm: a jelly-like fluid and the parts in it, except the nucleus.

8
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:34.947
The parts inside are called organelles.

9
00:00:36.027 --> 00:00:38.827
An organelle is a small part that does a special job.

10
00:00:40.107 --> 00:00:42.207
The nucleus holds the cell's instructions.

11
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.127
The ER is a network of folded membranes.

12
00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:50.087
It helps make and move many proteins and fats.

13
00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:55.710
Mitochondria release energy stored in food, such as sugar and fats.

14
00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.310
They release it in a form the cell can use.

15
00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.830
A vacuole is a sac that stores water, food and wastes.

16
00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.210
Plant cells have one large vacuole.

17
00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.810
Many animal cells have only small sacs.

18
00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:15.213
Now give the cell one job: replacing a worn-out protein.

19
00:01:16.453 --> 00:01:18.293
The nucleus holds the instructions.

20
00:01:19.713 --> 00:01:22.373
The ER helps make the new protein and move it.

21
00:01:23.513 --> 00:01:26.633
The mitochondria release the energy that building uses.

22
00:01:27.393 --> 00:01:29.973
The membrane lets in food and oxygen.

23
00:01:32.317 --> 00:01:33.857
Four parts, one job.

24
00:01:34.697 --> 00:01:37.137
Every life job needs a team like this.

25
00:01:38.017 --> 00:01:42.017
Growth, repair and response each need several parts working at once.

26
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.280
So what if only the mitochondria stop?

27
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.340
Does only energy use slow down, or do other jobs slow down too?

28
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.640
Pause and decide.

29
00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:00.847
Other jobs slow down too.

30
00:02:02.107 --> 00:02:04.287
The nucleus and the ER are still fine.

31
00:02:05.507 --> 00:02:09.067
But building needs energy, so the repair path slows and stalls.

32
00:02:10.627 --> 00:02:12.287
Growth and response slow down with it.

33
00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:16.827
No part decides anything; each one simply follows chemistry.

34
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.800
Here is the second idea.

35
00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.440
Picture the cell from above, as a floor plan.

36
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:25.780
Each part is a room.

37
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.140
Together, the rooms make up the whole cell.

38
00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:34.123
So when one room takes a big share, the others take smaller shares.

39
00:02:35.683 --> 00:02:39.023
Cells with different jobs often hold their parts in different amounts.

40
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:42.553
A heart muscle cell works without a break.

41
00:02:43.753 --> 00:02:46.573
It releases a lot of energy, mostly from fats.

42
00:02:47.913 --> 00:02:50.253
So mitochondria fill a large share of its space.

43
00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:53.173
A white fat cell stores fat.

44
00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:56.913
So one big fat drop fills most of its space.

45
00:02:58.213 --> 00:03:00.733
The other parts sit in a thin rim around the edge.

46
00:03:01.733 --> 00:03:04.913
Many of the same parts, very different floor plans.

47
00:03:06.173 --> 00:03:08.973
A part's size alone does not tell you a cell's job.

48
00:03:10.173 --> 00:03:12.813
But comparing parts with jobs can show a pattern.
